      "problem": "Solve the inequality $-2x-8\\le 4.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-8 & \\le 4 \\\\ -2x-8+8 & \\le 4+8 \\\\ -2x+0 & \\le 12 \\\\ -2x & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-2.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,12 \\\\ \\frac{-2x}{-2}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{12}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}x}{\\cancel{-2}}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-6 \\\\ x\\,\\, & \\ge \\,-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -6.$",
